  "id": "155",
  "title": "A closing ends a session that holds a state and is ignored by one that holds none",
  "spec": "PROTOCOL.md#1310-the-rooms-life-as-a-client-sees-it",
  "notes": "`issued` is an order between two states, so a receiver with nothing to compare against cannot tell a fresh closing from a replayed one, and obeying it would let a relay drive a joiner out of a live room with one frame. The same bytes therefore do two different things in one vector: ignored before the state, obeyed after it. A closing that does not verify is refused like any other frame and ends nothing (§13.2), and a client that ends on its own timer rather than on the verification is what `any-closing` removes.",
